{{smaller|'''Vinous fermentation''', the fermentation by which must becomes wine, as distinguished from ''acetic fermentation.''—'''Vinous hydromel, liquor''', etc. See the nouns.}}

'''vint''' (vint), ''v. t.'' [< ''vintage'', assumed to be formed from a verb *''vint'' + ''-age''.] To make or prepare, as wine.
{{smaller block|I wouldn’t give a straw for the best wine that ever was ''vinted'' after it had lain here a couple of years.
{{right|''Trollope'', Barchester Towers, xxi.}}}}

'''vintage''' (vin′tạ̄j), ''n.'' [Altered, by association with ''vintner'', from ME. ''vindage'', ''vendage'', < OF. ''vendange'', ''vindange'', F. ''vendange'', < L. ''vindemia'', a gathering of grapes, vintage: see ''vindemial''.] '''1.''' The gathering of the grapes; the season of grape-gathering; the grape-harvest. ''Blount.''
{{smaller block|The ''vintage'' time . . . is in September.
{{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 40.}}}}
'''2.''' The annual product of the grape-harvest, with especial reference to the wine obtained.
{{smaller block|The antient mythology seems to us like a ''vintage'' ill pressed and trod. {{right|''Bacon'', Moral Fables, vi., Int.}}

A sound wine, Colonel, and I should think of a genuine ''vintage.'' {{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Elsie Venner, vii.}}

The so-called ''vintage'' class, which are the finest wines of a good year kept separate and shipped as the produce of that particular year. {{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XXIV. 608.}}}}
'''3.''' Wine in general. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Whom they with meats and ''vintage'' of the best
And milk and minstrel melody entertain’d.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Lancelot and Elaine.}}}}

'''vintage†''' (vin′tạ̄j), ''v. t.'' [< ''vintage'', ''n.''] To crop or gather, as grapes, at the vintage.
{{smaller block|I humbly beseech his majesty that these royal boughs of forfeiture may not be ''vintaged'' or cropped by private suitors. {{right|''Bacon.''}}}}

